# ============================================================================
# .env.example — vLLM settings for vllm_xpu/compose.yaml (validated on the B60)
# ============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` in THIS folder (gitignored), then uncomment a variable to
# override. Compose auto-reads `.env` from the compose file's folder — no
# extra flags needed. From this folder:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   docker compose config    # preview the rendered command
#   docker compose up -d     # apply (recreates the container if it changed)
#
# Every variable is VALUE-ONLY — flag names live in compose.yaml's command,
# values come from here.
#   * Section 1: flags already in the command. Uncomment + edit to override;
#     the compose defaults equal the validated B60 recipe, so without a `.env`
#     (or with everything commented) nothing changes.
#   * Section 2: optional flags NOT in the command. Put the ones you want in
#     VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS; compose appends them to the command.
#   * Section 3: settings for your host — image, port, restart, group IDs.
#
# Only flags relevant to the Arc Pro B60/B70 (single GPU, 24/32 GB) are listed.
# Verified against vLLM v0.21.0 (intel/vllm:0.21.0-ubuntu24.04).
# The values were measured on the B60. This engine hasn't been tested on the B70
# yet; what differs per card is in ../INTEL_ARC_B60.md and ../INTEL_ARC_B70.md.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. FLAGS IN THE COMMAND (defaults = the validated B60 recipe)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# HuggingFace model to serve. Must fit the card: ~22.7 GiB usable on the B60,
# ~30 GiB on the B70 (e.g. openai/gpt-oss-20b MXFP4 ~13.7 GiB;
# Qwen3-30B-A3B-GPTQ-Int4 ~17 GiB).
# Swapping the model requires swapping the parsers below to match.
# Default: openai/gpt-oss-20b
#VLLM_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b

# Model name published on /v1/models (what gateways/UIs must request).
# Default: gpt-oss-20b
#VLLM_SERVED_MODEL_NAME=gpt-oss-20b

# Context window in tokens. Sizes the reserved KV pool on the card, so it is
# VRAM-coupled. Default is now the model's native max (131072 / 128k): it fits
# the 24 GB B60 because vLLM V1 chunked prefill bounds activation + compile
# buffers by chunk size (not context length), and gpt-oss's sliding-window(128)
# layers halve per-request KV cost — the measured pool held 179,589 tokens =
# 1.37x a single 128k request even at the old 0.75 util. Lower it (e.g. 65536)
# only to reclaim pool for concurrency; do NOT raise the util to compensate.
# Default: 131072
#VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=131072

# Fraction of VRAM vLLM budgets for weights + KV pool. 0.80 is the default now
# that the desktop is OFF the B60 (displays on the AMD iGPU): no compositor
# competing for VRAM, and an engine OOM just restarts the container instead of
# freezing the machine. torch.compile/Inductor buffers are NOT capped by this
# value — on the B60, 0.86 filled the card to 22.67/22.71 GiB -> OOM-edge
# instability, so treat 0.86 as a hard ceiling there and stay clear of it.
# (0.75 was the prior display-shared default.) The B70's edge isn't measured.
# Default: 0.80
#VLLM_GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION=0.80

# Reasoning parser — MODEL-COUPLED, not a preference. gpt-oss needs
# openai_gptoss (exposes the harmony analysis channel as message.reasoning);
# Qwen3-family models need qwen3.
# Default: openai_gptoss
#VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=openai_gptoss

# Tool-call parser — MODEL-COUPLED. gpt-oss needs openai; Qwen3 needs hermes.
# Default: openai
#VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=openai

# --host 0.0.0.0 / --port 8000 / --enable-auto-tool-choice are fixed in the
# compose file (the container port is also baked into the healthcheck; the
# host side is VLLM_HOST_PORT in section 3).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. OPTIONAL FLAGS — not in the command
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Put the flags you want in VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS, on one line separated by spaces;
# compose appends them to the command. Put single quotes around a JSON value,
# e.g. --speculative-config '{"model":"...","num_speculative_tokens":3}'.
# A flag already in the command belongs in section 1 instead.
# Default: empty
#VLLM_EXTRA_ARGS=--max-num-seqs 16 --enforce-eager
#
# Flags worth knowing on a single Arc Pro card:
#
# --max-num-seqs 16
#   Max concurrent sequences scheduled per step. The engine default targets
#   server farms; on a single-card, single-user box, lowering it shrinks
#   activation buffers and leaves more real headroom. Verify actual effect on
#   this build. vLLM default: engine-chosen (~256)
#
# --max-num-batched-tokens 16384
#   Max tokens processed per engine step (prefill chunk size). Bigger = faster
#   long-prompt ingest (RAG), at the cost of peak activation memory on the
#   card. vLLM default: engine-chosen (~8192)
#
# --kv-cache-dtype fp8
#   fp8 halves KV memory -> roughly double the context/concurrency per GiB of
#   pool. UNPROVEN on the Arc/XPU build — boot-test and check output quality
#   before adopting (same caution as the old FP8 weight bug).
#   vLLM default: auto (model dtype)
#
# --kv-cache-memory-bytes 4000000000
#   Gives the KV pool an EXPLICIT byte size; vLLM then sizes the pool from it
#   instead of from the util fraction. Sidesteps fraction maths, useful on
#   this XPU build precisely because of the uncapped-compile-buffer gotcha
#   above. vLLM default: unset (sized via the util fraction)
#
# --quantization fp8
#   Online (load-time) quantization for models that are NOT pre-quantized.
#   The Arc use case: dynamic FP8/INT4 to fit a bigger BF16 checkpoint onto
#   the card during a model swap. Do NOT set for gpt-oss-20b (already MXFP4)
#   or for *-GPTQ/-AWQ checkpoints (already quantized).
#   vLLM default: unset (checkpoint's native format)
#
# --enforce-eager
#   Disables torch.compile (MANDATORY on the llm-scaler fork for gpt-oss;
#   optional here — slower, but skips compile warm-up/buffers).
#
# --no-enable-prefix-caching
#   Disables KV reuse across shared prompt prefixes (ON by default; leave it
#   on for chat/RAG).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. HOST SETTINGS
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image. Leave it commented to follow the version this repo pins; a value here
# keeps you on it even after the repo moves on. After any image change, clear
# the compile cache once (see VLLM_XPU.md "Upgrading the image").
#VLLM_IMAGE=intel/vllm:0.21.0-ubuntu24.04
#
# Host port for the API. Without an address it listens on every interface.
# Put your host's LAN address in front to listen on that network only, e.g.
# 192.168.x.x:8000, and open the port to your subnet in the firewall. Clients
# on this host then need that address too, containers that use
# host.docker.internal included, and so do ./smoke.sh and ./bench.sh:
#   VLLM_ENDPOINT=http://192.168.x.x:8000 ./smoke.sh
#VLLM_HOST_PORT=8000
#
# What Docker does after a crash, a reboot or a Docker upgrade.
# unless-stopped brings this engine back on its own; "no" leaves it down until
# you start it, so a stopped engine can't fight another one for the GPU.
#VLLM_RESTART=unless-stopped
#
# GIDs of the host's render and video groups, which reach the GPU without
# root. They differ per host; check yours with: getent group render video
#RENDER_GID=992
#VIDEO_GID=44

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THE MODELS LIVE (defaults = Docker's named volume, run as root)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# To keep them in a folder you own, set BOTH lines below. Use the same values
# in every engine folder's .env, because the engines share the weights.

# Host folder for the weights. Must be an absolute path; `~` isn't expanded.
#HF_CACHE=/home/you/models/hf

# User the engine runs as: your `id -u`:`id -g`. The default 0:0 is root, so
# anything it downloads into your folder would be owned by root again. The
# GPU doesn't need root; group_add in compose.yaml passes render and video.
#VLLM_USER=1000:1000

# Empty = anonymous. Only needed for gated repos; neither block above is gated.
#HF_TOKEN=
